WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:03.510
Your workspace does not live alone.

2
00:00:03.510 --> 00:00:08.923
Mail, repositories, search, your own
internal tools — this episode walks the doors

3
00:00:08.923 --> 00:00:12.950
to the outside world, and the
discipline built into each one.

4
00:00:14.150 --> 00:00:18.312
Integrations connect once
and serve the whole workspace:

5
00:00:18.312 --> 00:00:21.507
Gmail, GitHub,
Shopify, Slack — and Tavily,

6
00:00:21.507 --> 00:00:22.845
already live here.

7
00:00:22.845 --> 00:00:26.932
Every agent and automation
borrows the same connection;

8
00:00:26.932 --> 00:00:29.310
nobody pastes keys into prompts.

9
00:00:30.510 --> 00:00:35.093
Open a connector and you can
read the door before you open it:

10
00:00:35.093 --> 00:00:41.302
exactly two operations — search and
extract — and an allowed-hosts list naming where

11
00:00:41.302 --> 00:00:42.485
requests may go.

12
00:00:42.485 --> 00:00:45.590
No hidden verbs, no surprise destinations.

13
00:00:46.790 --> 00:00:51.935
And here is what a connection buys:
remember deep research from episode two?

14
00:00:51.935 --> 00:00:55.455
It exists in this
workspace because Tavily is bound.

15
00:00:55.455 --> 00:00:59.990
Connect a door once, and a whole
capability lights up for everyone.

16
00:01:01.190 --> 00:01:04.134
For everything without
a ready-made connector,

17
00:01:04.134 --> 00:01:06.886
there is MCP — the
open protocol for tools.

18
00:01:06.886 --> 00:01:10.278
Point Tale at your internal
wiki, your ticket system,

19
00:01:10.278 --> 00:01:15.270
anything that speaks it, and its tools
appear to your agents like native ones.

20
00:01:16.470 --> 00:01:19.277
But native-looking is not native-trusted.

21
00:01:19.277 --> 00:01:25.028
An external tool is a new trust boundary,
and each one carries its own approval flag

22
00:01:25.028 --> 00:01:28.109
— flip it, and every
call waits for a person,

23
00:01:28.109 --> 00:01:30.710
exactly like the card in episode five.

24
00:01:31.910 --> 00:01:34.398
The last door is the one code opens.

25
00:01:34.398 --> 00:01:39.858
When agents run code, it runs in a
sandbox whose network is closed by default —

26
00:01:39.858 --> 00:01:43.936
allowed destinations are
named, everything else is refused,

27
00:01:43.936 --> 00:01:45.871
and the policy fails closed.

28
00:01:45.871 --> 00:01:50.710
A tool that cannot phone home
quietly is a tool you can afford to run.

29
00:01:51.910 --> 00:01:55.994
That is the pattern at every
door: operations you can read,

30
00:01:55.994 --> 00:01:59.178
hosts you can name,
approvals you can require,

31
00:01:59.178 --> 00:02:01.116
networks that default to no.

32
00:02:01.116 --> 00:02:06.030
Connect boldly — because each door
was built to be opened deliberately.

33
00:02:07.230 --> 00:02:12.256
Integrations, MCP, and the
boundaries that make them safe to use.

34
00:02:12.256 --> 00:02:18.750
Next episode: the people side — members,
roles, and teams, and who gets to see what.

35
00:02:19.950 --> 00:02:25.207
The integrations and governance sections
of the documentation carry every detail.

36
00:02:25.207 --> 00:02:26.830
See you in episode eight.

